{
  "suite": "pow",
  "protocolVersion": 1,
  "description": "sha256-v1 proof of work. Valid cases use the lowest nonce per sub-puzzle. Generated salts are the first 16 bytes of SHA-256(\"rg-vector-\" + index), base64url. solutions may be any valid nonces; these are the lowest. Each invalid case breaks one MUST-reject rule; where possible its solutions are valid proof of work under the lax reading (lenient base64, zero-padded or truncated salt, wrapping u8 index, coerced or u64-wrapped nonces), so a verifier that skips that rule accepts it. The difficultyBits 33 case carries nonce 14085872180, which meets 33 bits at sub-puzzle 0 of the 8-bit known salt (found offline, checked with node:crypto), so only the 1..32 range rule rejects it. The two out-of-range nonces were found with node:crypto: the first even value at or above 2^53, and the smallest k >= 1 for which -k wrapped to u64, that meets 8 bits at sub-puzzle 1 of the 8-bit known salt. The nonce-is-true and nonce-is-null cases (8 bits, count 1) each use the salt made of the first 16 bytes of SHA-256(\"rg-vector-coerce-true-\" + j) or SHA-256(\"rg-vector-coerce-null-\" + j), for the smallest j >= 0 where nonce 1 (Number(true)) or nonce 0 (Number(null)) meets 8 bits at sub-puzzle 0, found with node:crypto; so a verifier that coerces nonces accepts them and only the integer type rule rejects them.",
